  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nWe propose an optimization approach based on simulated annealing with focus on reducing the autocorrelation peaks. The central idea is to start from a good initial candidate sequence (the parent sequence) and gradually modify it by injecting \"mutations\" that reduce the objective value. Unlike traditional gradient-based methods, simulated annealing allows occasional moves to worse positions to maintain exploration of the search space. The justification is that:\n1. The parent sequence provides a plausible starting point that likely requires minimal or no scaling.\n2. The step function has discrete peaks that need to be reduced, which might not be captured well by derivatives.\n3. We need a method that can escape local minima while maintaining progress.\n\nOur implementation will:\n- Fix the sequence length to 128 (given by height_sequence_1 dimensions)\n- Read the parent sequence if available and use it as the base candidate\n- Implement a mutation operator that reduces any autocorrelation peak above a threshold by a fixed amount\n- Use simulated annealing with exponential cooling\n- Track the best solution found at any point\n\nMathematical specification:\nWe are minimizing f(a) = (2 * n * max(convolve(a,a))) / (sum(a))\u00b2\nWith n=128, we define:\n    Let conv(a) be the autocorrelation via full convolution (length 249)\n    Let peak_penalty = max(0, conv(a)[j] - C) for the peak we're targeting\n    We wish to reduce the objective value by maximizing the denominator or reducing the peak\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Initialize best_sequence with the parent sequence (if available) or uniform initialization\n2. Set temperature T0 (large) and cooling rate (0.95)\n3. In the main loop:\n   a) From current temperature, propose a mutation by:\n      - Identifying the maximum peak in conv(a)\n      - Lowering it by a fixed amount (\u03b1)\n      - For example, set conv_idx = idx_of_max(peak), then set a[conv_idx] = max(0, a[conv_idx] - \u03b1)\n      - Actually, by analyzing the problem we need to move away from all peaks, not just the maximum, so we'll:\n        * Choose a random index j\n        * Reduce a[j] by a small amount, but also consider that a[j] should pair with nearby large peaks to form the autocorrelation peak\n   b) We must check that a[conv_idx] reduction doesn't create negative numbers\n   c) Choose a random subvector to modify (not just the peak) to maintain the non-negative constraint\n   d) Calculate score = 2*n*max(conv)/sum\u00b2\n   e) With probability of crossover: accept the change if it improves the score\n   f) Otherwise: accept if  e^(dT) >= random, where dT is the temperature decrement\n\nSearch choices and useful variations:\n1. Mutation strength \u03b1 is chosen as 0.05 * (peak value) to prevent overshooting boundaries\n2. Step function has a fixed length (128)\n3. We use the parent sequence as the initial candidate\n\nComputebudget and robustness:\n1. Time: 1800 seconds\n2. Each evaluation: Convolution and score calculation\n3. We set timeout checks at the beginning of each search phase\n\nValidation and return contract:\n1. Check that non-negative numbers override to 0 if negative appear\n2. Only return valid sequences with sum > 0.01\n3. After timeouts or invalid operations, return a fallback uniform sequence (np.ones(128))",
